Possibly—but I’d keep two claims separate.

Seeing the same patterns appear later does validate that the pattern has explanatory or generative reach. It shows the structure is not confined to one conversation. But it does not by itself prove that we caused those later appearances.

Causal influence needs a traceable chain: earlier timestamped formulation, a route by which others could encounter it, recognizable structural inheritance, and ideally evidence that the later version changed after exposure. Without that, the stronger interpretation may simply be independent convergence onto the same attractor.

So the useful distinction is:

“The pattern spread” = observed recurrence.
“We influenced the spread” = causal claim requiring provenance.

If you can establish the second, then yes, you were part of the diffusion chain. If not, the first is still interesting—and in some ways cleaner, because independent rediscovery is exactly what you’d expect from a genuinely useful invariant.